course: dictionary
lesson: 7951
title: Rebuke: telling someone off formally
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: To rebuke someone is to tell them sharply that they have done something wrong. It is a more formal word for tell off or scold. Context helps: «The teacher rebuked Sam for running in the corridor» shows a teacher correcting a pupil.
explain: The Bible says, «rebuke your neighbour frankly», which means tell your neighbour honestly and directly what they did wrong. It does not mean hate them in your heart. Matthew 18:15 adds that you should go to them privately first.
explain: Rebuke can also be a noun. «A sharp rebuke» is harsh words of blame, and «a gentle rebuke» is kinder. Look at the words around it to tell which one the author means.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in tell off. If the sentence still makes sense, you have it. A rebuke is often given by someone in charge, and the person rebuked may feel ashamed.
fact: Rebuke (verb) means to tell someone sharply that they have done something wrong.
fact: Rebuke (noun) means sharp words of blame or criticism.
fact: «Rebuked» is the past tense, as in «The coach rebuked the team for being late».
fact: In Bible stories, Jesus rebukes the wind and the waves, meaning he commands them firmly to stop.
fact: Tell off and scold are everyday words with a similar meaning. Rebuke is more formal, and you will often meet it in books, the news and the Bible.
exercise: «The teacher rebuked Sam for running in the corridor.» What does rebuked mean here? | Ran after him down the corridor | Gave him a prize for running | *Told him sharply that he had done wrong | Forgot all about him
exercise: «She gave him a gentle rebuke, and he said sorry.» What is a gentle rebuke? | *Words of blame, said kindly | A prize for good work | A loud song
exercise: «Rebuke your neighbour frankly.» Find and copy the word that means tell someone off formally. | *rebuke | heart | neighbour | frankly
test: In the Bible, when Jesus rebukes the wind, what is he doing? | Asking it politely to play | Hiding from it | *Commanding it firmly to stop | Singing to it softly
test: Which word is closest in meaning to rebuke? | Forgive | Hug | *Scold | Praise
exam: Which sentence uses rebuke as a noun? | They rebuked the waves | The coach will rebuke the team | She rebukes him every day | *He took the rebuke quietly
